{
  "dom_id": "questionContainer",
  "name": "Question 31",
  "problem": "Given that the sequence ${a}_{n}$ is defined by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={\\begin{aligned}0,\\,n=1 \\\\ 1,\\,n=2 \\\\ -2,\\,n\\ge 3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ndoes ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ converge or diverge? If it converges, what is the sum of the series?",
  "worked_solution": "Let's compute the first few partial sums:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{1} & =0 \\\\ {s}_{2} & =0+1=1 \\\\ {s}_{3} & =1-2=-1 \\\\ {s}_{4} & =-1-2=-3 \\\\ {s}_{5} & =-3-2=-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the sequence of partial sums, we can see that the formula for the $n$th partial sum for $n\\ge 3$ is\n\n$$\n{s}_{n}=1-2(n-2)=5-2n.\n$$\n\nThe sequence of partial sums is divergent:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{s}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(5-2n)=-\\infty \n$$\n\nTherefore, the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ diverges.",
